{{short description|Pyrotechnic device used at parties}}
{{distinguish|text=the party drug [[poppers]]}}
[[File:Party poppers.jpg|thumb|Party poppers]]

A '''party popper''' is a handheld [[pyrotechnics|pyrotechnic]] device commonly used at [[party|parties]]. It emits a loud popping noise by means of a small, [[friction]]-actuated explosive charge that is activated by pulling a string.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://oxforddictionaries.com/definition/english/party%2Bpopper?q=party+popper|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303231456/http://www.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/english/party+popper?q=party+popper|url-status=dead|archive-date=March 3, 2016|title=party popper|publisher=Oxford Dictionaries|access-date=2013-05-10}}</ref> The explosive charge comes from a very small amount of [[Armstrong's mixture]] (a highly sensitive [[explosive]]) in the neck of the bottle-like shape.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.pyrouniverse.com/consumer/howtheywork.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150808175631/http://www.pyrouniverse.com/consumer/howtheywork.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=2015-08-08|title=How Fireworks Work|author=Colin Bradley|publisher=Pyro Universe|access-date=2013-05-10}}</ref> In some party poppers, the explosive charge is replaced by [[compressed air]]. In party poppers with an explosive charge, there are less than {{convert|0.25|gr|g}} of explosive. The streamers are non-flammable for safe use. The charge or compressed air blows out some [[confetti]] or streamers and emits a popping sound. The charge is often composed of red [[phosphorus]] and strong oxidizer, such as [[potassium chlorate]] and [[potassium perchlorate]].

There are also party popper [[revolver]]s on the market, which use a [[Speedloader]] — style cartridge filled with six-party popper charges inserted into a normally colourful plastic device loosely resembling a pistol or revolver.<ref>1150 - PARTY POPPER REVOLVER (bigfireworks.com)</ref> Its functionality is very much the same as a pistol; the depression of the trigger apparatus rotates the chamber so that a live charge is presented to a hammer, which falls onto a regular cap ring embedded in the bottom of the chamber. The chambers are one-time use only.

== Safety and precautions ==
{{Tone|section|date=February 2016}}
[[File:Better shot of the party popper.jpg|thumb|Contents being launched from the popper]]


Party poppers have been known to cause serious eye trauma and other facial injuries when aimed at people. Consumers are advised to avoid disassembling party poppers. Supervision of children during usage is also highly important. <ref>{{Cite news |date=2011-11-17 |title=Sarathkumar sustains a minor injury on left eye |language=en-IN |newspaper=The Hindu |url=http://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/Madurai/sarathkumar-sustains-minor-injury-on-left-eye/article2633495.ece |access-date=2016-03-11 |issn=0971-751X}}</ref> Party poppers are classified as "indoor [[fireworks]]," and as such, they are subject to legal restrictions in some places. Party poppers cannot be sold to anybody under the age of 16 in the [[United Kingdom]]. <ref>{{cite web |author=Lorraine Conway |date=2024-12-16 |title=Regulation of fireworks |url=https://researchbriefings.files.parliament.uk/documents/SN05704/SN05704.pdf |access-date=2025-01-01 |publisher=[[House of Commons Library]]}}</ref>

==Emoji==
The party popper has been part of [[emoji]]s (🎉) since 2015. It is also known as the "tada" emoji.<ref>{{cite web |title=🎉 Party Popper Emoji |url=https://emojipedia.org/party-popper/ |website=Emojipedia |access-date=29 April 2022 |language=en}}</ref>

